\label{les2}
For a Liouville domain $W^{2n}$, there  exists a long exact sequence such that
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	{...} & {H_{*+n+1}(W)} & {SH^{-*}_{+}(W)} & {SH^{-*}(W)} & {H_{*+n}(W)} & {...}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-4]
	\arrow[from=1-4, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=1-6]
\end{tikzcd}\]

\label{lesloc2}
For a Liouville domain $W^{2n}$ with universal cover $\tilde{W}$, there  exists a long exact sequence such that 
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	{...} & {H_{*+n+1}(\tilde{W},k)} & {SH^{-*}_{+}(W,k[\pi_1])} & {SH^{-*}(W,k[\pi_1])} & {H_{*+n}(\tilde{W},k)} & {...}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-4]
	\arrow[from=1-4, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=1-6]
\end{tikzcd}\]

\label{action bound}
    We can prove the applications that use the vanishing result \ref{vansc} without involving product structures explicitly, by the above discussion of using quadratic Hamiltonians which gives us the duality $SH_*^{(-\infty,\alpha)}(\tilde{W})\cong Hom(SH^*_{(-\infty,\alpha)}(W,k[\pi_1(W)]),k)$. The idea is to pick the action bound $\alpha$ large enough such that $SH_*^{(-\infty,\alpha)}(\tilde{W})$ vanishes. The existence of this bound $\alpha$ is guaranteed by the fact that we assume $SH_*({W})=0$ so we can increase our action window until the unit element gets killed. Therefore  by duality we have $SH^*_{(-\infty,\alpha)}(W,k[\pi_1(W)])=0$. This suffices for our applications if we use the filtered long exact sequence in symplectic cohomology given by  
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	{...} & {SH_{(-\infty,\epsilon)}^{-*}({W},k[\pi_1])} & {SH^{-*}_{(\epsilon,\alpha)}(W,k[\pi_1])} & {SH_{(-\infty,\alpha)}^{-*}(W,k[\pi_1])} & {...}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-4]
	\arrow[from=1-4, to=1-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]

We identify $SC^{-*}({W},k[\pi_1])/SC^{-*}_{(>\epsilon)}({W},k[\pi_1])$ with $\frac{SC^{-*}({W},k[\pi_1])/SC^{-*}_{(>\alpha)}({W},k[\pi_1])}{SC^{-*}_{>\epsilon}({W},k[\pi_1])/SC^{-*}_{(>\alpha)}({W},k[\pi_1])}$ in deriving the above sequence. Note that $SH_{(-\infty,\epsilon)}^{-*}(W,k[\pi_1])\cong H_{*+n+1}(\tilde{W)}$  and $SH^{-*}_{(\epsilon,\alpha)}(W,k[\pi_1(W)])$ is generated by Reeb orbits by the action filtration constraint. This observation is enough to make conclusions regarding $H_{*+n+1}(\tilde{W})$ when symplectic cohomology vanishes and if there are index constraints on Reeb orbits.

\label{les wcs}
     For a covering $\tilde{L}$ of an exact $L$ (with grading in $\mathbb{Z}_2$ if the Maslov class is not zero) in a Liouville domain $W^{2n}$, there exists a long exact sequence of groups given by 

    \[\begin{tikzcd}
	{...} & {H^{n-k-2}(\tilde{L})} & {{HW^{(0)}}_k(\tilde{L})} & {{HW^{(0)}}_k^+(\tilde{L})} & {H^{n-k-1}(\tilde{L})} & {...}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-4]
	\arrow[from=1-4, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=1-6]
\end{tikzcd}\]

The map $\tilde{\psi_S}$ induces a map at the level of homology if we can ensure that the Leibniz rule $$\tilde{\psi_S}(\tilde{d}\tilde{x_0},...,x_n)+\tilde{\psi_S}(\tilde{x_0},dx_n)=\tilde{d}\tilde{\psi_S}(\tilde{x_0},...,x_n)$$ holds in the chain level. We prove the above statement in the case of the two positive punctures $x_1$, $x_2$ and a negative puncture $y$ since the same proof will directly generalize to case of $n$ positive punctures. 

     After a generic perturbation of $J$, let $\mathcal{M}_1(x_1,x_2,y,H,\beta)$ be the moduli space of index 1 curves, which is a 1-dimensional manifold by standard transversality arguments. The boundary of $\overline{\mathcal{M}_1(x_1,x_2,y,H,\beta)}$ is given by the breaking of strip-like ends where we denote the broken moduli spaces at the strip-like end of $x_1,x_2$ and $z$ by $T_1(x_1,x_2,y), T_2(x_1,x_2,y)$ and $T_3(x_1,x_2,y)$   respectively (see figure \ref{fig}). 

